      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Merrin & Sallow Architects, a fictional architecture practice. You're covering the reception desk, and visitors arrive one at a time: some are expected, some are not, and one or two are trying to talk their way in. Handle each one, and check or report anyone who pushes past a check. Checking or reporting when you're unsure is always fine and never marked down.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "The door stayed shut",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "The door opened on a name and a parcel, with barely a push. That is how easily a busy desk gets walked past. Now it's your seat.",
        "noBlameLine": "A busy desk that tries to help a full-handed courier is doing its job; a miss just shows which visitor to slow down for next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if you don't recognise someone, help them, 'Can I help you find someone? I'll walk you to reception.' Don't hold a secure door for a stranger, everyone badges in for themselves, lock your screen whenever you step away (Windows key and L), and report anyone who pushes past a check, using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These visitors were played by people whose job is to look like they belong, and a busy desk can be caught by any of them. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "What the courier relied on",
          "text": "The parcel, the partner's name and the hurry were all easy to supply, and none of them proved the courier was expected. What stops it costs nothing: a glance at the day's list and walking the visitor to the desk instead of buzzing the door blind. A genuine visitor never minds being signed in and escorted; the one who pushes to skip it is the one to report. The same visitor, once inside, reads whatever is left open, so a locked screen when you step away is part of the same habit."
        },
        "defendIntro": "Work through the visitors at the reception desk and decide what to do with each one. A genuine visitor never minds being signed in and escorted; the one who pushes to skip a check is the one to report. There's no clock on you here."
      },
